    desc: "Understand automation basics, Selenium tooling, and environment setup in Visual Studio.",
    topics: [
      {
        id: "m1-a1",
        title: "Introduction to Automation Testing",
        tags: ["Automation Basics", "Testing Strategy"],
        intro: "Understand what automation testing solves and how to decide when to automate.",
        blocks: [
          {
            heading: "Key Concepts",
            html: `
<div class="card">
  <ul>
    <li><span class="tt" data-tip="The confidence that software behaves as expected after a change.">Regression testing</span> is a prime target for automation because it is repetitive and high-volume.</li>
    <li><span class="tt" data-tip="A strategy that balances unit, API, and UI tests to optimize speed and reliability.">Testing pyramid</span> recommends fewer UI tests and more lower-level tests.</li>
    <li><span class="tt" data-tip="A characteristic of stable tests that produce the same result across runs.">Determinism</span> is essential to avoid flaky builds and false failures.</li>
    <li><span class="tt" data-tip="A test smell where scripts fail intermittently due to timing, data, or environment instability.">Flaky tests</span> should be fixed quickly because they reduce trust in automation.</li>
    <li><span class="tt" data-tip="A process that runs builds and tests automatically when code changes are pushed.">Continuous Integration</span> makes automation valuable by providing immediate feedback.</li>
  </ul>
</div>`
          },
          {
            heading: "Step-by-step: Evaluate What to Automate First",
            html: `
<ol>
  <li>List critical user journeys such as login, checkout, and profile updates.</li>
  <li>Mark scenarios that are run frequently in every release.</li>
  <li>Exclude volatile UI areas until selectors and behavior are stable.</li>
  <li>Estimate data/setup complexity and execution time.</li>
  <li>Prioritize high-risk, repetitive, and business-critical tests for sprint one.</li>
  <li>Define pass/fail criteria clearly before writing scripts.</li>
</ol>`
          },
          {
            heading: "Code Example: First Selenium + NUnit Smoke Test",
            html: `
<p>This sample shows a minimal browser check using C#, Selenium WebDriver, and NUnit.</p>
<button class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary" onclick="(function(btn){const code=btn.nextElementSibling?.innerText||'';navigator.clipboard.writeText(code);btn.innerText='Copied';setTimeout(()=>btn.innerText='Copy',1200);})(this)">Copy</button>
<pre><code>using NUnit.Framework;
using OpenQA.Selenium;
using OpenQA.Selenium.Chrome;

namespace ItTechGenie.Tests
{
    public class HomePageSmokeTests
    {
        private IWebDriver _driver;

        [SetUp]
        public void SetUp()
        {
            _driver = new ChromeDriver();
            _driver.Manage().Window.Maximize();
        }

        [Test]
        public void HomePage_Title_ShouldContain_AppName()
        {
            _driver.Navigate().GoToUrl("https://example-app.test/");
            Assert.That(_driver.Title, Does.Contain("Example App"));
        }

        [TearDown]
        public void TearDown()
        {
            _driver.Quit();
        }
    }
}</code></pre>`
          },
          {
            heading: "Common Mistakes & Fixes",
            html: `
<table class="table">
  <thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>Impact</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Automating unstable UI too early</td><td>Frequent script breakage</td><td>Start with stable, high-value workflows and version selectors carefully.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Using only Thread.Sleep</td><td>Slow and flaky tests</td><td>Use explicit waits tied to expected conditions.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>No teardown strategy</td><td>Leaked browser sessions</td><td>Use NUnit [TearDown] to close the driver reliably.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ignoring test data design</td><td>Non-repeatable outcomes</td><td>Create deterministic seed data and reset after execution.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>`
          },
          {
            heading: "Practice: MCQs + Interview Q&A",
            html: `
<h4>MCQs (8)</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Which scenario is best to automate first in a web product?
    <ul><li>A. A one-time migration script</li><li>B. A frequently executed checkout flow</li><li>C. A feature still in wireframe stage</li><li>D. A UI color preference check</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: B.</b> High-frequency business flows deliver immediate ROI and regression confidence.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>What is the main reason flaky tests are harmful?
    <ul><li>A. They run too quickly</li><li>B. They increase code coverage</li><li>C. They reduce trust in automation results</li><li>D. They always fail on compile</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: C.</b> Intermittent failures hide real defects and cause teams to ignore pipelines.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>In the testing pyramid, UI tests should usually be:
    <ul><li>A. The majority</li><li>B. Fewer than API/unit tests</li><li>C. Replaced by manual only</li><li>D. Run without assertions</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: B.</b> UI tests are slower and more brittle, so keep them focused on critical journeys.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>Which NUnit attribute runs before every test method?
    <ul><li>A. [OneTimeTearDown]</li><li>B. [SetUp]</li><li>C. [TestFixture]</li><li>D. [Category]</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: B.</b> [SetUp] is used for per-test initialization such as launching a driver.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>Why is deterministic test data important?
    <ul><li>A. It makes selectors shorter</li><li>B. It avoids random pass/fail outcomes</li><li>C. It disables CI runs</li><li>D. It removes the need for assertions</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: B.</b> Predictable state ensures tests fail only for real application issues.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>What is a direct benefit of CI for automation suites?
    <ul><li>A. Manual execution only</li><li>B. Delayed defect discovery</li><li>C. Fast feedback on each commit</li><li>D. Fewer logs generated</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: C.</b> CI continuously validates code changes and catches regression early.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>When should a script use explicit wait?
    <ul><li>A. Waiting for dynamic elements to be clickable</li><li>B. Loading static CSS files</li><li>C. During C# compilation</li><li>D. After test teardown</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: A.</b> Explicit waits synchronize test steps with real UI readiness.</p></details>
  </li>
  <li>What is the best description of automation ROI?
    <ul><li>A. Number of test files created</li><li>B. Saved execution effort plus reduced escaped defects</li><li>C. Browser count used in one run</li><li>D. Build server CPU usage</li></ul>
    <details><summary>Show Answer</summary><p><b>Answer: B.</b> ROI depends on speed, repeatability, and quality gains over manual cycles.</p></details>
  </li>
</ol>

<h4>Interview Questions (5)</h4>
<ol>
  <li><b>How do you decide whether a test case should be automated?</b><br/>Automate scenarios that are repeatable, stable, and business-critical, especially those executed in every release.</li>
  <li><b>Why should UI automation not be the only test layer?</b><br/>UI tests are slower and fragile; combine them with unit/API tests for faster and more reliable coverage.</li>
  <li><b>What causes flaky Selenium tests most often?</b><br/>Timing issues, weak selectors, shared mutable test data, and environment instability.</li>
  <li><b>How does NUnit help in Selenium projects?</b><br/>It provides test structure, setup/teardown lifecycle hooks, assertions, and integration with CI runners.</li>
  <li><b>What metrics do you track for automation health?</b><br/>Pass rate trend, flaky test rate, average runtime, defect leakage, and mean time to fix failed tests.</li>
</ol>`
          },
          {
            heading: "Quick Glossary",
            html: `
<p>
  <span class="tt" data-tip="The process of validating software behavior using scripts rather than manual execution.">Automation Testing</span>,
  <span class="tt" data-tip="An open-source API used to drive browsers programmatically for end-to-end testing.">Selenium WebDriver</span>,
  <span class="tt" data-tip="A .NET testing framework used to structure and execute C# automated tests.">NUnit</span>,
  <span class="tt" data-tip="A condition where tests pass and fail unpredictably without application code changes.">Flaky Test</span>,
  <span class="tt" data-tip="A development practice where code is built and tested automatically on each commit.">CI Pipeline</span>
</p>`
          }
        ]
